== 2. the short section against the two folded sections
      [not word for word in a folded section] The half with the most people is not the same half.
      [not word for word in a folded section] It holds 92.7% of the world's people, and on the population maps I used its best centre has moved: it is in the western Alps in every year I tried up to 2010, and near Berlin from 2020 on (the two later years I tried, 2025 and 2030, are projections).
      [not word for word in a folded section] The difference is a sliver on each side of the world.
      [not word for word in a folded section] The half centred near Berlin reaches Manila, Kuala Lumpur and Singapore; the half centred in the Alps reaches São Paulo, the south of Brazil and most of Ecuador instead.
      [word for word in: people] In 1975 the South American sliver held more people, 41 million against 30 million.
      [word for word in: people] By 2020 the Asian one did, 92 million against 87 million.
      [not word for word in a folded section] The lead in 2020 is thin: between 2.6 and 4.9 million people, depending on the map, out of nearly 8,000 million.
      [not word for word in a folded section] A map of that half had been published already: Bill Rankin's "Human Hemisphere" of 2015, for the people of the year 2000, with its centre in western Switzerland.
      [word for word in: people] The two do not disagree, because they are for different years.
      [not word for word in a folded section] A search of the web found nobody who had said that the centre has moved, which is not proof that nobody has.
      [word for word in: woodpeckers] This study began with a few random words that had been sent to me, and one of them was woodpeckers.
      [not word for word in a folded section] Going by where woodpeckers have been recorded, and counting a kind found on both sides of the cut in part, the half with the most kinds of woodpecker holds about 83% of them.
      [word for word in: woodpeckers] It is centred in Gascony, in the south-west of France, give or take 300 km.
      [not word for word in a folded section] Each is told in full in its own section below.
